# Line-ending discipline.
#
# Without this, git falls back to core.autocrlf, which differs per machine. A
# contributor on Windows with autocrlf=true would get CRLF in the working tree
# and a whitespace-only diff on every file touched; one with autocrlf=false would
# get the opposite. Neither is a real change, and both are easy to commit by
# accident.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary files itself; `eol=lf` normalises text to LF
# in the repository and checks it out as LF on every platform, including Windows.
# This also matches check_eol.py / the org-wide .gitattributes, so the repo and
# the workspace tooling agree.
#
# The pattern and its attributes must be on ONE line -- written as a bare `*`
# followed by `text=auto eol=lf` on the next, git parses them as two (invalid)
# patterns and applies neither, which `git check-attr` reports as "unspecified".
* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.tar binary
*.gz binary
*.bz2 binary
*.xz binary
*.7z binary
*.rar binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.avi binary
*.webm binary
*.age binary

# Private keys must never be line-ending converted: some tools reject CRLF in a
# PEM, and a mangled key is unrecoverable.
*.pem text eol=lf
*.key text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that genuinely need CRLF. None of the current scripts
# require it (PowerShell reads LF fine), but keep the escape hatch documented so
# a future .cmd/.bat is not fought with.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf